I'm not interested in being to compute the result of recursively
applying one of these 'operators' to another.

But when x is borderline broda, I would like to be able to say
whereabouts on the borderline, relative to the territory on
either side of the border.

Has the train crossed the border? If none of it has, then the
answer is clearly No. If all of has, then the answer is clearly
Yes. If some but not all of has, then the answer (for some
purposes) is Sorta. If only the very front of the train has
crossed the border then it is "sorta-but-much-closer-to-being-in-X-
than-to-being-in-Y".

> So, what happens with a man, Joe, and a 13' x 16' room with the only 
> door in the middle of a 16' wall? What are the readings in various 
> areas for "Joe is in(side) the room." I'm assuming that the factors 
> that make for truth here are distances from the center of the door 
> sill on the inside (the origin), that the door frame is 4" deep, and 
> that, for the moment, angle from the origin is not significant. 

If he is fully inside the room, then his distance from the entrance
gives you the "slightly/barely"--"very" scale = ja'aru'e--ja'acai.

If he is part in and part out, then he is on the Sorta = ja'a cu'i
scale, and his position on the scale is determined by how much is
inside and how much outside ("sorta-but-somewhat-closer-to-being-
inside-than-outside", etc.).

